Below is a concise list of all schematic symbols that you will need to build a minimal, functional circuit around the **MCP4921 12‑bit DAC** with an SPI interface.  
The list includes every type of component that typically appears in such a design – power rails, decoupling, pull‑ups, and the DAC itself – and it follows the exact library names that exist in the KiCad libraries you mentioned.

| Symbol | Library | Typical use in the circuit |
|--------|---------|----------------------------|
| **MCP4921** | `Analog_DAC` | The 12‑bit DAC with SPI pins (VDD, GND, SCK, SDI, CS, LDAC, DOUT) |
| **VCC** | `Device` | Supplies the DAC (and any other logic) – connects to the VDD pin of the MCP4921 |
| **GND** | `Device`  | Common ground – connects to the GND pin of the MCP4920 and all other grounds |
| **R** | `Device`   | Generic resistor – used for the CS pull‑up, optional series resistors on SPI lines, or any biasing |
| **C** | `Device`    | Generic capacitor – used for decoupling (e.g., 0.1 µF between VDD and GND) and optional filtering |
| **L** | `Device`     | Generic inductor – not strictly required for a simple DAC circuit, but listed because the instruction says “include all related symbols, even simple components” |

> **Why these symbols?**  
> * The MCP4921 requires a supply rail, ground, and several signal pins.  
> * Decoupling capacitors are essential for stable operation.  
> * A pull‑up resistor on the CS line keeps the DAC in a known state when no master is driving it.  
> * The generic R, C, and L symbols cover any additional passive components you might add later (e.g., series resistors on SPI, RC filters, or an inductor for EMI suppression).  
> * Power symbols (VCC, GND) are mandatory for any schematic that uses a powered device.

---

